# Baltimore oriole

The Baltimore oriole is a brilliant orange and black icterid of eastern North
America, arriving from the tropics in late spring. The female plaits a deep,
pendulous pouch from plant fibre, horsehair and shreds of bark, knotting the
first strands around a forked twig and working downward until the chamber hangs
free, sometimes sixteen centimetres deep. The pouch swings clear of the branch
at the tip of a slender elm or maple limb, where few climbing predators will
follow. Adults take caterpillars, fruit and nectar, and will come to a cut
orange. See [[icteridae]].
